{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789239846,"nonce":"bVMQBe/Pyl71d548kcC/60csjLM=","title":"a field write through a Dynamic receiver stores into a slot resolved by name on another class","message":"Conformance Issue3635 passes on macOS and crashes on Linux (CI) with\n`malloc_consolidate(): invalid chunk size` then SIGABRT — the row behind\nthe \"always one less in CI\" score for weeks. Two defects, both silent on\nmacOS:\n\n1. `dB.key = { x: 10 }` with `dB:Dynamic\u003cB\u003e`: the field write path\n   (mir/field/lvalue.rs) had no Dynamic case, so it fell to the by-name\n   slot fallback (`resolve_field_index_by_name`), found `key` on some\n   unrelated class, and emitted a fixed-slot store (`gep [3]`) into an\n   anonymous object that has no such slot. glibc notices the overwritten\n   chunk header at the next consolidate; macOS's allocator never does.\n   Reproduce on macOS with `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES=/usr/lib/libgmalloc.dylib`\n   (guard malloc turns the out-of-bounds store into a SEGV).\n   `MallocScribble` / `MallocGuardEdges` did not catch it.\n\n2. `dB = {}`: the parser reads empty braces as an empty block, which lowers\n   to null. In a value position whose expected type is Dynamic or an\n   anonymous structure it is Haxe's empty object declaration, so every\n   field later written into it went nowhere.\n\nFix: a Dynamic receiver never takes the slot path — it goes to the\nreflective write (`haxe_reflect_set_field`) and the value is boxed by the\nassignment's RHS type (`pending_store_value_ty`), since a handle and a box\nshare a register type. A register with a class hint (derived clone,\nwrapper return) keeps its slot. The typer rewrites `{}` to an empty\nobject literal when `expects_object_value()` (expected_arg_type_stack /\nexpected_new_type_hint through TypeAlias/Optional to Dynamic/Anonymous);\nfunction bodies and `if (c) {}` stay blocks.\n\nGoing reflective exposed Issue7674 (`abstractProperty` of an abstract\nresolved through the by-name slot before): a property of an abstract has\nno slot and reads and writes through its accessor methods\n(`abstract_property_accessors`, keyed by the property symbol; writes\nconsult it first, reads last, because the symbol is shared with a\nsame-named field of the underlying class — `Int64.high` over\n`__Int64.high` — whose slot must keep winning).\n\nVerified on the NUC (Linux): Issue3635/7674/2254/4885 PASS.","files":null}]}